 "findings": {
  "low_level": [
   "Same session as W4_chunk4 (v4 build, commit 0ba868b00d): 118 (bf16) and 121 (fp32) registers, 3344 and 3848 SASS instructions for two calls against 2928 and 3456 for the binary search, no spill, no stack, same shared memory (66848 and 83344 bytes with the harness buffers), occupancy 2."
  ],
  "algorithmic": [
   "4-ary merge path (6 steps of three probes per side against 12 steps of one) against W4_chunk4 in one section at N 8192 k 2048: bf16 5035 against 4250 (0.84x), fp32 7387 against 6581 (0.89x), throughput 467 against 525 and 304 against 319. Against B3_tuned in a second section: bf16 5039 against 6498 (1.29x), fp32 7389 against 7113 (0.96x). Halving the dependent steps does not pay at 256 threads: 36 probe loads per thread instead of 24 through one shared memory pipe shared by 8 warps, and the select cascade and three address computations per step."
  ],
  "negative": [
   "The k-ary search is a loss at block scope at a range of 2048, as it was at warp scope at ranges up to 4 x IPT in the July merge sort study (WARP_MERGE_SORT_RESULTS.md section 14.1): the probes are bandwidth, not latency, when 8 warps search at once."
  ],
  "surprises": [
   "The search phase at block scope is bandwidth bound even though each thread's probe chain is a dependent latency chain: 256 threads x 2 loads per step keep the shared memory pipe busy enough that more probes per step cost more than they save."
  ],
  "open_questions": [
   "Fewer probes per thread (a shuffle-shared split among the lanes of a warp, which search neighbouring diagonals) rather than more is the direction for the block search."
  ]
 },
